==Korean==

===Etymology 1===
{{ko-etym-native|gy|家稀}}

{{ko-etym-native|wc|가히〮|kàhí|hangul=y|dot=,}} and subsequently as {{okm-l|개〯|kǎy}} in the 16th century, after the ''h'' has dropped.<ref>{{cite book|last=Lee|first=Ki-Moon|last2=Ramsey|first2=S. Robert|title=A History of the Korean Language|publisher=Cambridge University Press|year=2011|isbn=978-0-521-66189-8|page=163–164}}</ref> 

The prefix derives from the noun. Compare {{ncog|en|bitch}}.

====Pronunciation====
{{ko-IPA|l=y}}
[[Image:YellowLabradorLooking_new.jpg|thumb|A dog (a [[Labrador]])]]
* {{ko-tone|L_reg}}

====Noun====
{{ko-noun|count=마리}}

# [[dog]]
#: {{syn|ko|견공(犬公)}}
# {{lb|ko|figurative}} [[someone]] who does the [[bidding]] of [[another]]

=====Usage notes=====
Some younger speakers prefer {{m|ko|강아지|t=puppy}} to refer to dogs of any age, to avoid the vulgar connotations of the prefix.

